    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and i'm having issues with stability in the high-acid phase. can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in preventing the oil and water from separating under those conditions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a powerful emulsifier, works by having both hydrophilic (water-loving) and lipophilic (oil-loving) parts. In your low-fat mayo, the lecithin molecules surround the tiny oil droplets, acting like tiny shields. This prevents the oil droplets from coalescing and separating from the aqueous phase, even when the acidity is high. It's basically keeping the oil and water from deciding to go their separate ways.",
