    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you go deeper into the hydrocolloid idea? what kind of gums or thickeners would work best to stabilize the interface without messing with the mouthfeel too much?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For stabilizing the interface without creating a gummy mouthfeel, you'll want to look at hydrocolloids that build viscosity primarily in the continuous (water) phase, rather than directly coating the oil-water interface excessively. Gums like Xanthan gum are often excellent starting points because they provide significant viscosity at low concentrations without adding much perceived thickness or drag.\n\nAnother option to consider is Locust Bean Gum (LBG) or Guar Gum, but these can sometimes lend a slightly more noticeable texture than Xanthan. The key is to use them at the absolute lowest effective concentration—just enough to provide steric hindrance and slow down droplet movement, which is what prevents coalescence.\n\nWhen you introduce a hydrocolloid, it essentially creates a dense, viscous \"soup\" around the oil droplets, making it much harder for them to bump into each other hard enough to merge, even if the lecithin shield is slightly compromised by the acid. It's a physical barrier, not a chemical one, which is why it often preserves mouthfeel better than trying to chemically fix the lecithin structure itself.",
      "source": "target",
